From a87308d31ee31dd72585aa8578e70b46942f64ee Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Reichenbach, Michael" <755327+Silthus@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 11:02:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] A lint rule as a totality oracle's detector

A scoped ban or a paved path over an external API is a rule the project's
linter already states as data, and no chokepoint can express it. This makes
such a rule the detector of a totality oracle, so it is total over a named
set and refuted red, then green, like any other detector. There is no count
and no stored list of known findings: what the lint config excludes is the
listed residual, and the bullet's over: names the same files.

- config: a `lint` key names each tool once, an argv array and the format
  of its report, `oxlint-json` or `ruff-json`.
- lintTotalityOracles (src/enforcement/lint.ts) runs each tool once, reads
  its report, and keeps the findings of each named rule by any id the tool
  gives it. Any finding fails. It is not run, never a pass, when the tool
  is not configured, cannot start, or writes a report that is not its
  format; when the tool says it linted no file of the whole project; and
  when a file did not parse, since a linter checks no rule there.
- run: the totality pass runs it over the whole project, and a lint via
  never reaches the batched test invocation; refute asks the same pass,
  unchanged.
- PostToolUse: each tool runs once, on the written files it lints, within
  20 s, and a finding is printed with the invariant, the site, and the two
  honest options. It appends no run, since the written files are not the
  named set.

The two format readers are tested on output recorded from oxlint 1.72.0
and ruff 0.16.9 (src/enforcement/lint-recordings), which the fixture's
lint command replays for the paths it is handed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
