+## A lint rule as the detector
+
+A totality oracle whose `via` reads `lint <tool>:<rule>` (with an optional
+`matching "<text>"`) runs a linter instead of a test. The config names each
+tool once, as an argv array and the format of its report:
+
+```json
+"lint": {
+  "oxlint": { "command": ["pnpm", "exec", "oxlint", "-c", "nodejs/.oxlintrc.nodejs.json", "--format", "json"], "format": "oxlint-json" },
+  "ruff": { "command": ["ruff", "check", "--force-exclude", "--output-format", "json"], "format": "ruff-json" }
+}
+```
+
+The run's totality pass runs each tool once over the whole project, however
+many bullets name it; `refute` asks the same pass. A finding is a diagnostic
+of that rule, known by any id the tool gives it
+(`eslint(no-restricted-imports)`, `no-restricted-imports`,
+`eslint/no-restricted-imports`; `TID251`, `banned-api`), whose message
+carries the matching text: oxlint's message and its help, ruff's message.
+Any finding is a fail. Otherwise it is not run, never a pass, when:
+
+- the tool is not configured, cannot start, or writes a report that is not
+  its format;
+- the tool says it linted no file of the whole project. oxlint always says
+  how many files it linted; ruff reports no count and warns only sometimes
+  that it found no Python file, so a ruff command aimed at the wrong folder
+  can still read as a pass;
+- a file did not parse (oxlint's diagnostic without a rule, ruff's
+  `invalid-syntax`). A linter checks no rule in a file it cannot parse.
+
+The set it is total over is the lint config's own. Its exclusion is the
+listed residual the bullet's `over:` names, and a scoped check hands the
+tool the files to check, so the command must honour that exclusion for a
+file it is handed: oxlint does, ruff does only with `--force-exclude`.
+Nothing is counted and nothing is stored beside the rule, so a new finding
+and an old one read the same: a fail, until it is fixed or the residual is
+named in both places.
+
